INT. HOSPITAL LAUNDRY ROOM - 4 A.M.

Steam. Rolling carts of folded scrubs. A row of industrial dryers thumping like slow hearts.

NURSE AGNES BRIGHTWATER (40s, braids pinned under a cap) leans on a cart, eyes closed, fanning herself with a chart. DR. SAMIR HADDAD (30s, resident, rumpled) sits on the floor with his back to a dryer, a granola bar in each hand. ORDERLY CHEN WEI (50s, thick glasses) folds towels with machine speed.

A figure stands in the doorway, uncertain. A slight woman in a visitor's lanyard, clutching a plastic bag of knitting. This is the VISITOR (60s).

VISITOR
Excuse me. I'm looking for the cafeteria.

AGNES
Wrong floor, hon.

VISITOR
They sent me down.

SAMIR
They send everyone down. It's the only warm room.

The Visitor steps in. Agnes gestures to a stool. The Visitor sits, rummages in her bag, pulls out a half-finished scarf, and begins knitting. Chen Wei folds a towel, glances at her, folds another. Samir unwraps a granola bar and chews. Agnes closes her eyes again.

VISITOR
I'm Dolores, by the way.

AGNES
Agnes.

SAMIR
Samir.

CHEN WEI
Chen.

DOLORES
Pleased to meet you all. I'm visiting my husband. Third floor. He had a stroke.

AGNES
I'm sorry.

DOLORES
Oh, he's stubborn. He'll be fine. He'll be furious about the hospital food.

SAMIR
Everyone is.

DOLORES
Do you have a husband, Agnes?

AGNES
Eighteen years.

DOLORES
That's a long time.

AGNES
It's about right.

Chen Wei sets down a stack of towels, sits on a cart, and watches Dolores's needles click. The dryers thump. Samir takes a second bite.

DOLORES
Would any of you like a scarf? I make them for the ward.

SAMIR
I'll take one.

CHEN WEI
Green.

DOLORES
Green it is.

AGNES
(eyes still closed)
I'll take whichever one he doesn't.
